2186093053 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092219 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092061 :question/id #uuid "63b5b1e6-1885-554c-973c-bd78555f44ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-5385"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad049d53-af38-5917-b9d0-be965cadabcb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354486}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given Some Information About Another Point"} :step/id #uuid "5d6ff88a-3d62-5c9d-954c-133cea8e9bc9" :step/math-academy-id 19867 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093133}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093133 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092273 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092112 :question/id #uuid "f0c5bd75-dd47-5028-8eff-38c87e7411bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-7116"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "386a4f1a-4ee5-5bf7-aff2-e190c83f4529" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256425} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351268}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Using a Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "d5283ef8-3450-5420-aefb-79ef34b80e84" :step/math-academy-id 26165}] :activity/title "Converting from Polar Coordinates to Cartesian Coordinates" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092435}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093080} :activity/id #uuid "63a5d964-3f68-5587-8159-19cf0b907bf0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050277 :topic/id #uuid "3c176860-3fcd-5563-b4a1-3988f6ed1c93" :topic/math-academy-id 998} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093080 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093356 :tutorial/content "Any curve defined using polar coordinates $(r(\\theta), \\theta)$ is a parametric curve in the parameter $\\theta.$ For this reason, we can use the formula for differentiating parametric curves to compute the derivative of a polar curve:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nAs an example, suppose we want to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r = \\sin4\\theta,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/5a/5a0d25e54c2d688341f4bc1d0046cf485008cd0e2556d6bf2b9c8ebf8c20b0e2.png)\n\nFirst, recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx(\\theta) = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y(\\theta) = r \\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $r=\\sin 4\\theta$ for this particular curve, we get\n\n$$\nx(\\theta) = \\sin 4\\theta \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y(\\theta) =\\sin4\\theta \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate $x'(\\theta)$ and $y'(\\theta)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta$ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(\\theta ) & =(\\operatorname{sin}4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{'} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}4\\theta )^{'}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}4\\theta \\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{'} \\\\  & =(4\\operatorname{cos}4\\theta )\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}4\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta$ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}(\\theta ) & =(\\operatorname{sin}4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{'} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}4\\theta )^{'}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}4\\theta \\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{'} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}4\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}4\\theta \\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{'}(\\theta )}{{x}^{'}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{4\\operatorname{cos}4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "68089c07-eb7d-5103-9642-9bfffd81f0eb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1119 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f4183a87-43cd-5726-865d-c40e01e75695" :step/math-academy-id 2202 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093193}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093081 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092236 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092088 :question/id #uuid "2a08ba95-78ee-5a32-b785-48f3a2dd037b" :question/math-academy-id "e-628"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2037eca-37ca-5750-9c36-b1613c66ff4f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092596} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321123}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Polar Curve"} :step/id #uuid "2683b5b0-e96e-5b05-b363-5a158544b9f7" :step/math-academy-id 2203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093193 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092310 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092157 :question/id #uuid "daec9915-9b7d-5029-9461-385ca8125d3f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9948"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0a0dd73-d526-529b-9a98-594e8d5825aa" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050531}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345060}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Polar Curve Given the Derivatives With Respect To X and Y"} :step/id #uuid "9a3c72d5-df39-548e-bf80-d6ade1ff4534" :step/math-academy-id 37474 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093194}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093194 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092311 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092158 :question/id #uuid "1c8315d1-78ec-5f4f-90f3-1f7cffff712a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9949"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c86c57df-5103-5931-b423-e4dafeb9721a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050848}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092760} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321087}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Polar Curve at a Point Given Derivatives With Respect To X and Y"} :step/id #uuid "17953d1f-0c65-543b-ab03-2d8d359a937e" :step/math-academy-id 37475 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093200}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093200 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092313 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091924 :question/id #uuid "47cd3aef-8196-5e95-93f9-14c5f2ec7a40" :question/math-academy-id "e-10034"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "823136b4-992e-5e49-87a1-3a415027d78a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049940}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321091}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Constant Polar Curve"} :step/id #uuid "0377fed7-a412-5fea-9e60-a9dbb0d0db98" :step/math-academy-id 37859 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093081}}] :activity/title "Differentiating Curves Given in Polar Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092469}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065168} :activity/id #uuid "43bcaf37-4f35-5441-9262-198383c30f89" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050789 :topic/id #uuid "671f0549-be03-552c-a435-a204b8c7e733" :topic/math-academy-id 305} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065168 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065335 :tutorial/content "The chain rule can be used to differentiate trigonometric functions like \n\n$$\ny = \\sin (2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that this function can be written as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = \\sin u, \\qquad u = 2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{sin}u)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}u\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $u=2x,$ we obtain the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\operatorname{cos}(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "40623a7d-e08a-5ec3-a3d6-1fac6307b20a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1275 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2bd9287a-2129-5eca-a08d-0604b85098f4" :step/math-academy-id 2508 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065169}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065169 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064281 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064125 :question/id #uuid "2bb17470-6365-5e08-b649-f9f42146a4c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-732"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "73216188-6524-55c3-b945-86d13ef64170" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051084}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491440} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297322} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491425} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491436}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "802f8bf9-59c4-5175-a0ca-7d35efead9be" :step/math-academy-id 2509 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065170}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065170 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064282 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064126 :question/id #uuid "7f56b401-c800-5708-8978-b84ce3f542f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-733"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ef81a0d-5a3a-5d7c-83e9-7d9ed0e8c5c6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050985}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329889}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Reciprocal Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "d9287e55-1147-562c-8f80-87d0d9025112" :step/math-academy-id 2511 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065171}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065171 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064283 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064127 :question/id #uuid "373a63fc-3dae-5880-8a49-63b5c64888f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-734"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ec11481-cea2-514b-aa78-707fcf8838cc" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490497} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490490}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Power of a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "be380263-f82e-517f-baaa-359929115440" :step/math-academy-id 2513 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065172}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065172 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064284 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064129 :question/id #uuid "4ebd0289-b278-5352-b7bd-10ae218c43fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-735"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048465}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484798} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484803}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "f87118f2-75ec-56c4-9391-82c2aa059a28" :step/math-academy-id 2515}] :activity/title "The Chain Rule With Trigonometric Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064405}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060534} :activity/id #uuid "10bdaba2-b2d6-5479-a66a-9f5c1d933300" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050902 :topic/id #uuid "6fa843c3-0803-5725-a7d6-d567e4f1b216" :topic/math-academy-id 281} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060534 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060897 :tutorial/content "The **second derivative**  is the derivative of a function's first derivative. It is written as $f''(x).$\n\nTo calculate the second derivative of a function $f(x)$, we first need to calculate the first derivative $f'(x).$ After that, we calculate the derivative of $f'(x).$ So using prime $\\left('\\right)$ notation, we can define the second derivative of a function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf''(x) = (f'(x))'.\n$$\n\nLet's try it out with the function $f(x) = 4x^2 + 3x.$ The first derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =(4{x}^{2}+3x)^{'} \\\\  & =4(2)x+3 \\\\  & =8x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now calculate the derivative of $f'(x)$ to give us $f''(x).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{''}(x) & =({f}^{'}(x))^{'} \\\\  & =(8x+3)^{'} \\\\  & =8+0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the second derivative of $f(x)$ is $8.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "947c462e-76d9-5248-93f8-a558f1ad16c7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2633 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9931d327-3dfa-546d-9bad-52bfcf096bda" :step/math-academy-id 12357 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060535}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060535 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059677 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059505 :question/id #uuid "0bc72287-b782-5add-89ef-f2a465573ab0" :question/math-academy-id "e-3653"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89f65f58-bd3d-5c3f-b9e0-462d74d75b59" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050420}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514409}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Second Order Derivatives"} :step/id #uuid "67b86b8b-be5c-54ba-b03c-e422581c1992" :step/math-academy-id 12358 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060536}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060536 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060898 :tutorial/content "We can also express the second derivative using $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d} x}$ notation, also known as Leibniz notation. If we have a function $f(x),$ then the second derivative $f''(x)$ can be written as\n\n$$\nf''(x) = \\dfrac {\\textrm d^2 f}{\\textrm d x^2} = \\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac {\\textrm df} {\\textrm d x}\\right).\n$$\n\nSometimes, we define a function as $y=f(x),$ in which case we could also express the second derivative as $\\dfrac {\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f21f7901-ec61-5c3d-a6f2-6bba03a55a17" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2634 :tutorial/title "Using Leibniz Notation"} :step/id #uuid "8fa38988-5cde-5291-bf68-d15bba187f2a" :step/math-academy-id 12359 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060537}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060537 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059678 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059506 :question/id #uuid "b092bc79-d369-5ff0-a6bb-5fc059699275" :question/math-academy-id "e-3654"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6afb4058-908a-5983-9c1e-78ee3fbadf44" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484417} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233603} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484415}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Second Order Derivatives Using Leibnitz Notation"} :step/id #uuid "65dcc063-d790-5cae-ba88-b2b94d516359" :step/math-academy-id 12360 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060538}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060538 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060899 :tutorial/content "To calculate the third derivative of the function $f(x)=2x^4+3x,$ we need to differentiate it three times.\n\nComputing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{4}+3x) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4{x}^{4-1}+1\\cdot 3{x}^{1-1} \\\\ \\text{ } & =8{x}^{3}+3{x}^{0} \\\\  & =8{x}^{3}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{''}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8{x}^{3}+3) \\\\  & =8\\cdot 3{x}^{3-1}+0 \\\\ \\text{ } & =24{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(24{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =24\\cdot 2{x}^{2-1} \\\\ \\text{ } & =48x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third derivative of $f(x)$ is $f'''(x)=48x.$ \n\nUsing Leibniz notation, the third derivative of a function $f(x)$ can also be written as\n\n$$\nf'''(x)=\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(f(x))\\right)\\right)=\\dfrac {\\textrm d^3}{\\textrm d x^3}f(x).\n$$\n\nIn general, if we want to calculate the $n$-th order derivative of a function, we need to differentiate it $n$ times:\n\n$$\nf^{(n)}(x)=\\dfrac {\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}f(x).\n$$\n\n**Note**: The parenthesis in the superscript of $f^{(n)}(x)$ means that it is the the $n$-th derivative of $f(x).$ This is to distinguish it from $f^n(x)$ with no parenthesis, which indicates the $n$-th power of $f(x).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f1bfdba2-25dc-51af-97f8-00b3b216d0a4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2635 :tutorial/title "Higher Order Derivatives"} :step/id #uuid "2c85fed7-69ed-56ea-8cfa-78cf775d9d35" :step/math-academy-id 12361 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060539}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060539 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059679 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059507 :question/id #uuid "627f1ef4-64a8-5764-89c7-cb4e31009dbe" :question/math-academy-id "e-3655"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "322168bb-52e3-5352-a2e3-c5bdbf2bc13e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048749}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233772} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317359}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Higher Order Derivatives"} :step/id #uuid "75f9df48-9a05-5828-82cb-52a03412297c" :step/math-academy-id 12362}] :activity/title "Second and Higher-Order Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059952}]}
